architecture-tutor: The module names the trust boundaries as the VPN edge, the ALB listener, and the security groups around the instances `[module]`.

At the **VPN edge**, the diagram shows the on-premises DC entering via two IPsec tunnels to the transit gateway and the engineer laptop entering via Client VPN (TLS) `[module]`. The boundary check from Module 3 is the tunnel/peer itself: only traffic that arrives through those VPN endpoints is admitted into the VPC routing domain; the VPN edge does not inspect the client certificate that the ALB later requires `[derived]`. At the **ALB listener** (`:443`, TLS + mTLS verify), the listener terminates TLS, presents the ACM server certificate, requests a client certificate, validates the chain against the Private CA anchor, and checks the CRL `[module]`. If that fails, the request stops at the listener and never reaches a target `[derived]`. At the **service instances**, the security group check from Module 2 is source-based reachability: traffic is allowed only from the ALB, so direct access that bypasses the listener is dropped `[module]`. The security group does not re-check the certificate, because the instance receives plain HTTP plus X-Amzn-Mtls identity headers that the ALB has already validated `[module]` `[derived]`.
architecture-tutor: Simplified: the **VPN edge** is the private-network door. In the diagram it is the two IPsec tunnels from the data center into the transit gateway, plus the Client VPN TLS path from the engineer laptop `[module]`. It checks that traffic arrived through one of those private tunnels; it does not inspect the client certificate `[derived]`.

The **ALB listener** is the identity checkpoint. It is the `:443` TLS + mTLS verify box, and it checks that the client certificate is valid, issued by the Private CA, and not on the CRL, then passes validated identity headers to the backend `[module]`. The **security groups around the service instances** are the last-hop check: they allow traffic only from the ALB, so anything not coming from the ALB is dropped. They do not re-check certificates, because the instance receives plain HTTP with X-Amzn-Mtls headers already validated by the listener `[module]`.
architecture-tutor: On Monday, a deployment issues a new Private CA client certificate with a 7-day validity and installs it on the engineer's laptop `[module]`. The previous certificate does not get revoked just because it was replaced; it stays valid until its own `notAfter`, which is at most 7 days after it was issued, and then expires `[derived]`. So the old certificate ages out naturally, and rotation stays routine rather than incident-driven `[module]`.

If a key is lost mid-week, say Wednesday, and the loss is reported, the CRL is the backstop `[module]`. The administrator revokes the certificate in Private CA, which puts its serial on the CRL `[general]`. The ALB listener in the diagram — the `:443` TLS + mTLS verify box — holds the CRL in its trust store and checks it during the handshake `[module]`. A request from the stolen laptop, traced as engineer laptop → Client VPN → ALB listener → target instance, therefore fails at the ALB listener and never reaches the service instance `[derived]`. If the loss was never noticed, the short 7-day validity still caps the exposure until the certificate expires `[module]`.
architecture-tutor: Starting at the **engineer laptop** in the diagram, the request goes over **Client VPN (TLS)** to the **transit gateway** — that crossing is the **VPN edge** trust boundary, admitting only traffic that arrives through that private tunnel `[module]`, and the VPN edge itself does not inspect the client certificate `[derived]`. The name `api.internal.example.com` resolves to the **ALB**, and the **ALB listener** (`:443`, TLS + mTLS verify) terminates TLS: it presents its ACM server certificate, requests the client certificate, validates the chain against the **Private CA bundle** anchor, and checks the **CRL** `[module]`. That is the **ALB listener** trust boundary. On success, the ALB forwards **plain HTTP + X-Amzn-Mtls identity headers** to a **target instance** in the private subnets `[module]`; reaching it crosses the **security groups around the instances**, which allow only ALB-sourced traffic `[module]`.

The return path reverses the same route: **service instance → ALB listener → transit gateway → Client VPN → engineer laptop**, passing back through the security group, the listener, and the VPN edge `[derived]`. After validation at the listener, every hop to the instance runs on private addresses inside the VPC `[module]`.
architecture-tutor: That scenario is outside the module, because the module takes the position that certificates are the only credentials and client authentication happens at the edge `[module]`. The following is general knowledge, not course material `[general]`.

In general, switching to OAuth2 tokens would change the ALB listener's identity checkpoint: instead of mTLS verify against a Private CA bundle and CRL, the listener or an authorizer would validate a bearer token (JWT signature/expiry, or token introspection), so the trust store and CRL pieces in the diagram would no longer be the revocation mechanism; token revocation/expiry would be handled by the authorization server `[general]`. The service instances would receive identity from token claims rather than X-Amzn-Mtls identity headers, and the application would authorize from those claims `[general]`. The VPN edge and security-group boundaries would still provide network-layer admission and reachability, but they would not validate the token; identity enforcement would sit only at the token-validation step `[general]`.
architecture-tutor: A route table is outside Module 7's supplied content; this is general networking knowledge `[general]`. A route table maps destination CIDR prefixes to next-hop targets (for example, an internet gateway, NAT gateway, transit gateway, or VPC endpoint). When a packet is sent, the route table looks for the most specific matching prefix; if several match, the longest prefix wins `[general]`. A default route is a catch-all route with destination `0.0.0.0/0` (IPv4) or `::/0` (IPv6) — used only when no more specific route matches `[general]`.

In the combined architecture, the route tables are not shown in the diagram, but they are what direct private-subnet traffic toward the NAT gateway for general outbound egress, and toward VPC interface endpoints for AWS API calls so that traffic does not use the NAT gateway `[derived]`.
architecture-tutor: A certificate chain is the leaf certificate presented during the handshake, followed by any intermediate CA certificates, ending at a root CA certificate that acts as the trust anchor `[general]`. The verifying party walks from the leaf upward, checking each signature against the issuing CA's public key and confirming the root (or an intermediate deliberately configured as the anchor) is in its trust store `[general]`. Validation also checks validity dates and typically name and usage constraints, and it checks revocation — in this architecture, via the CRL in the ALB trust store `[general]` `[module]`.

In the combined architecture, the ALB acts as the validating party for client certificates: it validates the client chain against the Private CA anchor and checks the CRL as part of the mTLS handshake `[module]`. So a presented certificate must chain to the Private CA anchor in the trust store and not be on the CRL; otherwise validation fails at the listener `[derived]`.
architecture-tutor: The module's ALB listener in the diagram is in **verify mode** (`:443`, TLS + mTLS verify) `[module]`. In verify mode the listener terminates TLS, validates the client certificate chain against the Private CA anchor in its trust store, checks the CRL, and then forwards plain HTTP with X-Amzn-Mtls identity headers to the target instance `[module]`. The service instance therefore receives a validated identity without having to perform certificate validation itself `[derived]`.

Passthrough mode is not what this architecture uses, so the following is general knowledge, not module content `[general]`. In passthrough mode, the ALB does not validate the client certificate chain or revocation status; it forwards the client certificate (or connection information) to the target, and the target is responsible for validation `[general]`. That would move the identity trust boundary from the ALB listener to the service instance, which would then need its own trust store and termination logic `[general]`.
